  "title": "Make Connections - Eigen Review",
  "body": " Make Connections - Eigen Review    \\begin{enumerate} \\item How do eigenvalues relate to the invertibility of a matrix? \\end{enumerate}     \\\\  Two convenient Sage tools. If A is a square matrix, then   A.eigenvalues() gives a list of the eigenvalues, and  (A - lambda*identity\\_matrix(n)).right\\_kernel() gives a basis of the null space if is the number of rows and columns of and is an eigenvalue. How does this help you find eigenvectors?      Consider the matrix .     Find the eigenvalues of and a basis for each of the eigenspaces.      Is diagonalizable? If so, find and and verify that .      Find a diagonalization of .      Find a diagonalization of .      Find and explain why equals the product of the eigenvalues of .       We know that the eigenvalues of are the roots of the characteristic equation .     If is an eigenvalue, what does this say about ?      What does this say about the invertibility of ?      How do the eigenvalues of a matrix tell us about its invertibility?       Last class we looked at the transition matrix applied to trucks at two locations. We wanted to know the long-term behavior of an initial state that evolves as . In other words, what happened to as grows large. We found that had eigenvalues and , and the . So if we start with 1000 trucks total so that , then in the long-term we have so there will be 750 trucks at location and 250 at location .  Let's look at a different transition matrix: .     Find the eigenvalues of and bases for its eigenspaces.      Find a diagonalization of .      What happens when you look at as grows?      What is different about the eigenvalues of and that distinguishes the long-term behavior?     "
