  "id": "labs-4",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "labs-4.html",
  "type": "Worksheet",
  "number": "",
  "title": "Lab 3, Due: October 23, 2026",
  "body": " Lab 3, Due: October 23, 2026    Instructions: Complete the following exercises in your groups. For the write up:     You should type up your report (a google docs version is available on Blackboard).    Include screenshots of your work in Sage.    You only need to hand in one report for your group. Only include the names of students who contributed.    This lab is graded out of 30 points. Each problem will be graded out of 5 points - you earn 5 points by including your reasoning, following all directions, and making only minor errors.     There is a page of Sage cells available here .  In this lab, we'll see how computer animators use matrix transformations to tell stores. In fact, when you watch a Pixar movie, you're actually seeing some very elegant applications of linear algebra.  Now, let me introduce you to Wooody, the lovable star of the animated kids' film Toy Tale due out this holiday season.   Introduction to Wooody from Toy Tale.   The computer animators who made Toy Tale assembled the film frame by frame. To make use think that Wooody is moving, they make changes in Wooody's position from one frame to the next. This is where linear algebra is useful. (Actually, Wooody really lives in a three-dimensional world and each part of his body may be moving in different ways so the situation is a little more complicated, but hopefully you will get the idea.)  If you go to this website , you will find a figure that can make Wooody move. On the left, you see a picture of Wooody, while on the right you will see a picture of him after a matrix transformation has been applied.  Here is the function we will consider; it is not quite as simple as what we have seen in class: The six sliders along the top of the diagram represent the quantities      Using homogeneous coordinates, we represent this change as      Compute this matrix product and verify that it produces the appropriate expressions for and .      The benefit of using homogeneous coordinates is because they introduce additional parameters and . What role do these two parameters play in the transformation?       Press the Reset button if you have modified Wooody. As shown above, Wooody is waving with his left hand. In the first scene of the movie, Wooody comes on screen and waves with his right hand. What is the matrix transformation that has been applied?   Wooody waving his right hand.       Next, Wooody begins his morning calisthenics and performs a cartwheel in the following frames reading across the first row and then the bottom.   Wooody doing a cartwheel.      What is the transformation that produces the first (upper left) frame?      Using , how can you produce the second frame (upper right)? What is the matrix that produces the second frame?      How can you continue using to generate and , the matrices that produce the remaining two frames?       At this point in the story, Wooody remembers a long-ago love and the camera moves in for a close up.   Wooody in love and close up.   There are two ways to do this. The first two frames are similar to previous questions. The third is a little tricky but the diagram gives you another tool to help. Once you have the diagram configured in some way you life, you may press the button Apply . The image on the left is set to that on the right and the transformation is reset to the identity. You may then operate on the new figure from scratch. (Mathematically, you are composing two functions\/transformations.)     Explain a way to make the sequence of frames above.      Determine the function that takes our original picture of Wooody into the final close up.       Wooody decides to go out for a walk. In the morning sun, he casts a shadow that looks like this:   Wooody's shadow on a walk.      Find the matrix transformation that creates the shadow. You might remember how we have found the matrix representing a matrix transformation by looking at what happens to the vectors and .      As the sun comes up, his shadow gets shorter.   Wooody's shadow gets shorter.   Explain what transformations achieve this and how you found them. (Think about composing transformations again.)     "
